WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
The drop shot for complete beginners.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.750
The drop shot isn't a regular baseline shot either.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.910
Think of every drop shot as a small tactical change.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:16.000 line:5%
Sign: observation area

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.570
For a drop shot to work, you first have to fool your opponent.

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:20.150
Set up as if for a normal drive, take the racket back, and fool your opponent into staying back.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.960
If your baseline game isn't strong enough or the disguise isn't good,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.500 line:5%
Banner: ball machines sold in 50+ countries · Banner: pioneer in smart sports equipment · Banner: market leader in ball machines

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.070
your opponent can easily run it down even if it goes over.

00:00:25.390 --> 00:00:29.550
For the technique, first train switching grips with one hand,

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:34.810
going from the forehand grip to a continental grip in an instant.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:40.110
At contact the racket face starts upright and slides from the back of the ball to underneath it.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:44.990
It's about absorbing the pace: imagine the ball sticking to the strings a little longer.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:48.150
A good drop shot doesn't travel forward after landing; it may even spin back a little.

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.060
Technically the drop shot isn't complicated; it's mostly that soft, absorbing feel.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:01:01.780
In practice, mix drives and drops, one drive then one drop, which is closer to how you'd use it in a match.

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:03.900
You train the feel, and you train fooling your opponent.
